C = [
("h1", "13. Conscience in Paul’s Letters"),
("p", "Claim: Paul uses the word conscience of what bears witness about a person’s own conduct. It can be weak, defiled, seared, good or pure. He never makes one believer’s conscience the rule for another’s, and in the chapter of Romans that most concerns the weak, he does not use the word at all."),
("sub", "13.1 The uses"),
("p", "The word appears in Paul’s letters, and in his own speeches in Acts, in a small number of places. They can be set side by side. In each Paul says whose conscience it is and what it does."),
("ctbl", "Conscience in Paul’s letters and speeches.", [
    ("Passage", "Whose conscience, and what it is said to be or do", "Setting"),
    ("Romans 2:15", "Of Gentiles who have not the law: “their conscience also bearing witness”", "Their thoughts accusing or excusing"),
    ("Romans 9:1", "Paul’s own: “my conscience also bearing me witness in the Holy Ghost”", "His statement of truth, reported as written"),
    ("Romans 13:5", "“for conscience sake,” as a reason for subjection", "Read in Study 77"),
    ("1 Corinthians 8:7, 10, 12", "The weak: “their conscience being weak is defiled”; emboldened; wounded", "Meat offered to idols"),
    ("1 Corinthians 10:25, 27, 28, 29", "“asking no question for conscience sake”; “conscience, I say, not thine own, but of the other”", "The market and the feast"),
    ("2 Corinthians 1:12", "Paul’s: “the testimony of our conscience”", "His conduct in the world"),
    ("2 Corinthians 4:2; 5:11", "Of every man and of the Corinthians: Paul commends himself and is made manifest in their consciences", "His ministry"),
    ("1 Timothy 1:5, 19; 3:9", "A good conscience; a pure conscience; some have put it away and made shipwreck", "The end of the commandment; faith"),
    ("1 Timothy 4:2", "“having their conscience seared with a hot iron”", "Those who depart from the faith"),
    ("2 Timothy 1:3", "Paul’s: “with pure conscience”", "His service to God"),
    ("Titus 1:15", "“their mind and conscience is defiled”", "The defiled and unbelieving"),
    ("Acts 23:1; 24:16", "Paul’s own words: “in all good conscience before God”; “a conscience void of offence”", "Before the council and before Felix"),
  ], [0.22, 0.50, 0.28]),
("sub", "13.2 What conscience does in these passages"),
("p", "In every place the conscience is attached to a person and to something that person has done or is doing. It bears witness (Romans 2:15; 9:1; 2 Corinthians 1:12). It accuses or excuses (Romans 2:15). It can be good, pure or void of offence (1 Timothy 1:5; 3:9; 2 Timothy 1:3; Acts 23:1; 24:16). It can be put away (1 Timothy 1:19), seared (1 Timothy 4:2) or defiled (1 Corinthians 8:7; Titus 1:15). It can be weak, emboldened or wounded (1 Corinthians 8:7, 10, 12). The word is about the inner witness to one’s own act. It is not a source of commands. Paul never writes that a believer is to do what his conscience tells him in the sense that conscience gives the rule. He writes of conscience as something that can be good, and that can be hurt."),
("sub", "13.3 Conscience is not the judge"),
("p", "Paul sets a limit on the conscience itself. “But with me it is a very small thing that I should be judged of you, or of man’s judgment: yea, I judge not mine own self. For I know nothing by myself; yet am I not hereby justified: but he that judgeth me is the Lord” (1 Corinthians 4:3, 4). A clear conscience (“I know nothing by myself”) does not justify him. The judge is the Lord. The passage is in a different part of the letter and about Paul’s ministry, not about meat. It shows that Paul does not treat the conscience as the final authority even over his own acts. The same man who says “I have lived in all good conscience before God until this day” (Acts 23:1) says that the Lord is his judge."),
("sub", "13.4 Whose conscience limits whose liberty"),
("p", "In 1 Corinthians 10:29 Paul writes, “Conscience, I say, not thine own, but of the other.” The verse says that in the case in view the conscience that limits the eater’s liberty is not his. The volume draws two points from it. The first is that Paul separates the believer’s own conscience from the other man’s. The second is that the believer’s liberty is bound by the other man’s conscience only in the case Paul names, where a man says “This is offered in sacrifice unto idols.” Outside that case the believer eats and asks no question, “for conscience sake” (1 Corinthians 10:25, 27). The conscience of the other man does not govern the believer everywhere. It governs where it has been made known and where the act would wound it."),
("sub", "13.5 Romans 14 does not use the word"),
("p", "The chapter of Romans that most concerns the weak and the strong does not contain the word “conscience.” It speaks of being “weak in the faith” (Romans 14:1), of being “fully persuaded in his own mind” (Romans 14:5), of “faith” (Romans 14:22, 23), and of one who “doubteth” (Romans 14:23). The volume notes the difference. Piper describes the stumbling block in terms of conscience: “enticing them to do what their conscience condemns.” The volume agrees that the description fits the case, since a man who eats against his persuasion acts against what he believes is permitted. It also notes that Paul’s terms in Romans are faith, mind and doubt, and in 1 Corinthians they are conscience and knowledge. It does not merge them into one doctrine of conscience."),
("sub", "13.6 Weak is not the same as wrong"),
("p", "A weak conscience in Paul is not a wrong conscience. The man of 1 Corinthians 8:7 has a weak conscience, and Paul does not tell him that his conscience is mistaken and must be overruled. He tells the strong not to wound it. Neither does Paul tell the weak that their weakness is a virtue. In Romans 14:1 he calls the man “weak in the faith,” and in 14:14 he says that nothing is unclean of itself. The weak man’s view of the meat is not Paul’s view. The weak man is nevertheless to be received, because God has received him. The volume says that Paul asks for the weak man’s conscience to be respected without being called right."),
("sub", "13.7 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Conscience in Paul is an inner witness to a person’s own conduct, and it can be good, pure, weak, defiled or seared.",
    "Paul does not make conscience the rule of conduct, and he does not make it the final judge of its own bearer.",
    "In 1 Corinthians 10:29 the conscience that limits the eater is the other man’s, in the case Paul names.",
    "Romans 14 uses faith, mind and doubt, and does not use the word conscience.",
    "A weak conscience is respected without being called right.",
  ]),
]
